A direct version is useful for understanding the pair condition. For every index `i`, the code checks all positions after it and adds one when `nums[i]` is larger. Equal values are not counted.

A dry run for `[3, 1, 2]` gives two pairs: `(3, 1)` and `(3, 2)`. The method also works for an empty array and for an already sorted array.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    long long int numberOfInversions(vector<int> nums) {
        long long inversions = 0;

        for (int left = 0; left < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++left) {
            for (int right = left + 1; right < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++right) {
                if (nums[left] > nums[right]) {
                    ++inversions;
                }
            }
        }

        return inversions;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the input copy made by the platform signature.

Values can be large, so the code first gives each different value a rank after sorting a copy of the array. Then it scans from right to left. The Fenwick tree stores how many values have already been seen for each rank.

For a value with rank `r`, the query for ranks below `r` tells how many smaller values are on its right. Duplicate values use `bisect` style lower-bound logic, so equal values are not counted.

For example, `[3, 1, 2]` is processed as `2`, then `1`, then `3`. The first two steps add zero and one, and the last step adds two, giving three? Wait, scanning right to left: `2` adds zero, `1` adds zero, and `3` adds two, so the result is two.

```java
class Solution {
    public long numberOfInversions(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] sorted = nums.clone();
        java.util.Arrays.sort(sorted);

        FenwickTree tree = new FenwickTree(n);
        long inversions = 0;

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            int rank = lowerBound(sorted, nums[i]) + 1;
            inversions += tree.query(rank - 1);
            tree.add(rank, 1);
        }

        return inversions;
    }

    private int lowerBound(int[] values, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = values.length;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (values[middle] < target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }

    private static class FenwickTree {
        private final int[] tree;

        FenwickTree(int size) {
            tree = new int[size + 1];
        }

        void add(int index, int value) {
            while (index < tree.length) {
                tree[index] += value;
                index += index & -index;
            }
        }

        int query(int index) {
            int total = 0;
            while (index > 0) {
                total += tree[index];
                index -= index & -index;
            }
            return total;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for sorting and Fenwick operations.

Space complexity: `O(n)`.

The segment tree stores counts of values already placed to the right. After coordinate compression, a query over ranks smaller than the current rank gives the new inversion count.

The array is read from right to left. An array such as `[4, 3, 2, 1]` produces six inversions, while an empty or one-element list returns zero naturally. The result uses `long` because the number of pairs can be larger than a 32-bit integer.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public long NumberOfInversions(List<int> nums)
    {
        int n = nums.Count;
        if (n == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        List<int> sorted = new List<int>(nums);
        sorted.Sort();

        SegmentTree tree = new SegmentTree(n);
        long inversions = 0;

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i)
        {
            int rank = LowerBound(sorted, nums[i]);
            inversions += tree.Query(0, rank - 1);
            tree.Update(rank, 1);
        }

        return inversions;
    }

    private int LowerBound(List<int> values, int target)
    {
        int left = 0;
        int right = values.Count;

        while (left < right)
        {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (values[middle] < target)
            {
                left = middle + 1;
            }
            else
            {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }

    private class SegmentTree
    {
        private readonly int[] tree;
        private readonly int size;

        public SegmentTree(int n)
        {
            size = 1;
            while (size < n)
            {
                size *= 2;
            }

            tree = new int[size * 2];
        }

        public void Update(int index, int value)
        {
            int node = index + size;
            tree[node] += value;
            node /= 2;

            while (node > 0)
            {
                tree[node] = tree[node * 2] + tree[node * 2 + 1];
                node /= 2;
            }
        }

        public int Query(int left, int right)
        {
            if (left > right)
            {
                return 0;
            }

            left += size;
            right += size;
            int result = 0;

            while (left <= right)
            {
                if ((left & 1) == 1)
                {
                    result += tree[left++];
                }

                if ((right & 1) == 0)
                {
                    result += tree[right--];
                }

                left /= 2;
                right /= 2;
            }

            return result;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the sorted values and segment tree.

